## Caching

Redis via [ioredis](https://github.com/redis/ioredis). Run one locally with `docker run -p 6379:6379 redis`, or set `REDIS_URL` in `.env` to point at a hosted instance. The connection is opened in `loaders/index.js` before the app starts (same pattern as the database modules), and closed gracefully on shutdown.

Most app code shouldn't call `redis.get`/`redis.set` directly — use the JSON-aware `cache` helper in `utils/cache.js` instead:

```js
import { cache } from "./utils/cache.js";

// Cache-aside: returns the cached value if present, otherwise runs the
// function, caches its result for 60s, and returns that.
const user = await cache.getOrSet(`user:${id}`, 60, () => db.findUser(id));

await cache.set("some:key", { hello: "world" }, 300); // TTL in seconds, optional
await cache.del("some:key");
```

`GET /healthz` reports live connectivity (`redis.status === "ready"`). Not using caching anywhere yet? This module is safe to leave installed and unused, or delete `config/redis.js`/`utils/cache.js` and the `ioredis` dependency entirely.

